  "throughline": "Most of [Nietzsche](/philosophy/thinker/nietzsche)'s books argue. This one preaches, sings, and tells a story. *Thus Spoke Zarathustra* is a philosophical novel dressed as scripture: a prophet comes down from a mountain to teach a humanity that does not want teaching, and the book follows him across four parts as his great doctrine forms, breaks him, and slowly gets affirmed. Nietzsche borrows the name of the ancient Persian prophet Zoroaster and the cadence of Luther's Bible on purpose, writing in deliberate mock-scripture so that the new gospel arrives in the rhythm of the old one it means to replace. The story is built around the death of God, meaning the idea that modern European culture has already stopped believing in a divine moral order whether or not it has admitted as much to itself, taken here not as an argument to be won but as an accomplished fact, the premise the whole book stands on. If the old foundation of values is gone, what now? Zarathustra's answers are the three ideas the twentieth century could not stop arguing about: the Übermensch, the figure who could create new values out of the wreckage; the last man, the comfortable nothing humanity might shrink into instead; and the eternal recurrence, the thought that everything you have lived returns forever, offered as the heaviest weight a life can be asked to bear. The book is also the single most misused thing Nietzsche wrote, drafted after his death into a politics he despised. Walked on its own terms, in its own voice, it is stranger and lonelier than the caricature: the story of a teacher who keeps failing to be understood, by his audience and very nearly by himself.",
