      "The world Zarathustra walks into is the one the West had lived in for two thousand years, in which the worth of a human life was guaranteed from outside it. Good and evil were not human inventions but features of reality, underwritten by God and answerable to heaven; the point of this life was to pass a test set elsewhere, and its sufferings were bearable because they were owed a reward in a world to come. On that picture the saint is the summit of humanity, the one who turns away from the body, the appetites, and the earth toward the eternal. The book takes that whole arrangement as already finished. It does not argue against God's existence. It treats the disappearance of God as a thing that has already happened to European culture, and asks the question almost nobody else was asking: what now?",
      "Zarathustra's first answer is a redirection, and it is the hinge of the break. Where the old picture pointed up and away, he points down and back, to the ground underfoot. In the Prologue he tells the crowd to remain true to the earth and to stop believing those who promise hopes beyond it. The meaning that used to be imported from heaven has to be made here or not at all. This is why the book is so physical, so full of bodies, mountains, animals, eating and dancing and climbing. The despisers of the body, the people who treat the flesh and the earth as a prison to escape, are among Zarathustra's named enemies, because their contempt for the here-and-now is the old heaven wearing a disguise.",
