          "p": "The book opens with a descent. Zarathustra, thirty years old, has gone up into the mountains to live alone with his eagle and his serpent, and for ten years he has been content in his solitude. Then one morning he wakes with the sun and decides his wisdom has grown too heavy to keep. Like a cup that wants to overflow, like the sun that would mean nothing without the eyes it shines on, he must carry what he has down to people. He calls this his going under. The German word Nietzsche uses, rendered by Thomas Common in his 1909 translation as \"down-going,\" is the same word used for the setting of the sun, and it is the structural engine of the whole book. To teach humanity, Zarathustra has to come down from the height, and coming down is also a kind of decline, an exposure, a risk. The prophet's first act is not to ascend but to descend, into a world that will not understand him."
