          "p": "The spirit begins as a camel, the beast of burden. The camel is the reverent type, the one that kneels down and asks to be loaded, that wants to bear the heaviest things and tests its own strength by how much weight it can take on. Zarathustra lists the burdens it seeks out: to humble oneself, to love those who despise us, to stand by hard truths, to suffer for the sake of knowledge. The camel is, in plain terms, the dutiful person who takes the inherited values with total seriousness and carries them into the desert, the scholar, the believer, the disciplined moralist who reveres what he has been given and labors under it. This is not contemptible. The camel's strength is real, and a spirit has to be able to bear weight before it can do anything else. But the camel only carries; it does not question who packed the load. Out in the loneliest desert, Zarathustra says, the first transformation happens."
