          "p": "The will to power has its own most dangerous misreading, and the book itself supplies the correction if it is read carefully. The crude version takes \"will to power\" to mean a lust to dominate other people, a charter for bullies and tyrants. That is not what the chapter describes. The deepest form of the will to power in Zarathustra's account is not power over others at all but power over oneself, the capacity of a living thing to overcome and surpass what it currently is. Life tells Zarathustra its real secret in exactly these terms: it is, in Common's phrase, that which must ever surpass itself. The will to power is first of all self-surpassing, which is why the chapter carries that title and not \"the will to dominate.\" Domination of other people is one shape the drive can take, and usually a poor and reactive one, the recourse of those who cannot expand any other way. The model Nietzsche admires is the artist mastering a hard form, the thinker conquering a problem, the person overcoming something in himself. To flatten the whole idea into wanting to boss people around is to mistake its weakest, most external form for the thing itself."
