          "p": "It also ties the speech straight back to the three metamorphoses and forward to the rest of the book. If life is that which must ever surpass itself, then the camel-lion-child sequence is not an arbitrary ladder; it is life's own basic motion played out in a single spirit. The human being who self-overcomes, who crosses the rope toward the Übermensch, is not violating its nature but expressing it most fully, doing on purpose and at the highest pitch what every living thing does blindly. And the line points to the book's hardest test still ahead. A spirit that lives by self-surpassing is always reaching past its present self, always saying that what is must be overcome, which is a kind of permanent No to the given. The looming question, the one that nearly breaks Zarathustra in Part III, is whether such a spirit can ever turn around and say Yes to the whole of what has been, including the parts it would rather have surpassed and left behind. That is where the will to power meets its limit, and where the book's deepest idea arrives."
